51	## Test strategy
52	
53	### Regression baselines and failure classification
54	
55	Run the closest existing test and the broadest practical baseline before changing
56	an upstream-owned behavior. Record pre-existing failures by exact test name and
57	failure mode so later runs can distinguish a regression from inherited state.
58	
59	When a broad suite fails, rerun the exact case independently before assigning a
60	cause. Record repeatable failures as regressions until disproved; record a
61	one-time timeout, deadlock, or infrastructure failure separately with the
62	isolated rerun result. A green focused suite does not erase a new repeatable
63	broad-suite failure.
64	
65	### Contract tests
66	
67	Test strong IDs, invalid/missing/stale identifiers, command errors, subscription lifetime, event coalescing, revision behavior, and fake-workspace history without linking the GUI. Test both successful and unsuccessful commands, and assert that reported results agree with authoritative state.
68	
69	### Real-adapter application tests
70	
71	A fake cannot validate `Plater`, `GLCanvas3D`, plate membership, `GUI_ObjectList` dependencies, event ordering, or Orca's undo stack. Add an application-level harness that loads deterministic project fixtures and covers:
72	
73	- project-ready signaling rather than fixed event-loop delays;
74	- a dedicated application name and bundle identity so UI automation cannot target another Orca build;
75	- an isolated temporary configuration and repository-owned fixture;
76	- deterministic on-bed object placement, active tab, selection, and camera-visible state before reporting readiness;
77	- object and viewport selection in both directions;
78	- rename, duplicate, remove, plate switch, import, undo, and redo;
79	- committed native transform and exact-value transform;
80	- callback snapshots at the advertised revision;
81	- subscriber destruction;
82	- project replacement and session-ID invalidation;
83	- operations initiated outside the adapter.
84	
85	The POC self-test is evidence and test-design input, not a production harness: its STL setup could not reproduce rename undo, and it intentionally terminated through `std::_Exit`.
86	
87	### Shell end-to-end tests
88	
89	Run the complete native application with a deterministic multi-plate project. Verify the visible real canvas, camera controls, selection, native gizmos, Slice, Check print, return to Prepare, undo/redo, object pane synchronization, Agent pane persistence, resize, maximize/restore, and the unchanged legacy layout when testing a compatibility mode.
90	
91	Visual appearance alone is insufficient. Validate state from authoritative callbacks and pair visibility claims with screenshots or rendered evidence.
92	
93	Use the harness to establish deterministic application state; use UI automation
94	for real pointer, keyboard, focus, and rendering checks rather than for fragile
95	startup setup. For canvas policy, inventory every overlay surface and verify
96	rendering and hit-testing separately. Compare a stock window with the product
97	policy, then exercise selection, orbit, pan, zoom, active gizmo input, slicing,
98	Preview, and restoration of the prior presentation state.
99	
100	### First launch against a staging server
101	
102	First launch looks for OrcaSlicer and JusPrin v1 in their default folders for
103	the platform (on macOS `~/Library/Application Support/OrcaSlicer` and
104	`.../JusPrin`), whatever `--datadir` says, and only reads them. Three variables
105	set up a test run on a throwaway data folder:
106	
107	- `JUSPRIN_SERVER_URL` names the JusPrin server and wins over
108	  `jusprin_server.base_url` in the settings file. Use it instead of writing
109	  the settings file before launch: the v1 import runs only when the data
110	  folder has no settings file yet.
111	- `JUSPRIN_V1_DATADIR` names another v1 folder; set to `""`, there is none.
112	- `JUSPRIN_ORCA_DATADIR` names another OrcaSlicer folder; set to `""`, there
113	  is none.
114	
115	Unset, the real folders are used. The shell and real-adapter harnesses point
116	both folder variables at a folder that does not exist, so they never read a
117	real install. On Windows an empty variable cannot be set; name a folder that
118	does not exist instead.
119	
120	```bash
121	# v1 import and the welcome-back window
122	D=/tmp/JusPrin2-stg; rm -rf "$D" && JUSPRIN_SERVER_URL=https://jusprinv2-stg.obico.io <app>/Contents/MacOS/OrcaSlicer --datadir "$D"
123	# a new person who has OrcaSlicer: the welcome, then the OrcaSlicer import
124	D=/tmp/JusPrin2-stg; rm -rf "$D" && JUSPRIN_SERVER_URL=https://jusprinv2-stg.obico.io JUSPRIN_V1_DATADIR= <app>/Contents/MacOS/OrcaSlicer --datadir "$D"
125	```
126	
127	### Printer tests without hardware
128	
129	Bambu paths (printer-menu Idle / Printing / Offline, Select Machine, print job)
130	are exercised by an in-process fake that substitutes for the Bambu printer
131	agent while a real Bambu Lab **system** preset stays selected. How to turn it
132	on, the JSON control file, isolated-instance rules for agents, and acceptance
133	checks: [Fake Bambu README](../../src/slic3r/GUI/JusPrin/Testing/README.md).
134	
135	Print-host paths (Moonraker and OctoPrint) run against the real host software
136	in Docker, with simulated printer boards:
137	[print hosts README](../../tests/printer_hosts/README.md). Do not add a fake
138	Bambu network layer (MQTT/FTPS/discovery) without an explicit request.
139	
140	### Platform matrix
141	
142	Before release completion, cover:
143	
144	- Windows with WebView2;
145	- macOS with WKWebView;
146	- Linux/X11 and supported Wayland configurations with WebKitGTK;
147	- supported CPU architectures and a physical GPU where applicable;
148	- 100%, 150%, and 200% scaling or equivalent platform DPI settings;
149	- light and dark mode;
150	- keyboard focus, global shortcuts, clipboard, text selection, and IME composition;
151	- WebView startup, local resource loading, reload, and recovery;
152	- streaming scroll anchoring while orbiting and resizing;
153	- idle and streaming CPU/memory observations.
154	
155	Classify localized CSS, focus, packaging, or backend problems as platform defects unless evidence shows they require a different architecture.
156	
157	### Software GL on Windows machines without a GPU
158	
159	A Windows machine with no graphics driver reports system GL 1.1, so the launcher
160	(`src/OrcaSlicer_app_msvc.cpp`) loads `<exe dir>\mesa\opengl32.dll` instead.
161	Nothing in CMake or this repository supplies that file or what it depends on, so
162	provision every freshly configured build tree with
163	`src/slic3r/GUI/JusPrin/Testing/windows-gl/provision-mesa-windows.ps1`.
164	Placement is not interchangeable:
165	
166	| file | goes | because |
167	| --- | --- | --- |
168	| `opengl32.dll` | `mesa\` subfolder | the launcher loads it by that path; beside the executable it shadows the system GL and the app dies at launch |
169	| `opengl32.dll` again | beside each `*_harness.exe` | a harness links the app in and has no launcher, so only a copy beside it replaces the system GL 1.1 |
170	| `libgallium_wgl.dll` | beside the executable | the `opengl32.dll` stub imports it, and Windows resolves imports against the process directory |
171	| `dxil.dll` | beside the executable | Mesa's `d3d12` driver signs translated shaders with it; Windows Server does not ship it, Windows SDKs carry it under `Redist\D3D\x64` |
172	
173	Each gap has its own silent signature:
174	
175	- **No `libgallium_wgl.dll`:** GL falls back to 1.1, the log has `glcontext not ready, postpone init` and no `got opengl version` line, and the canvas stays black for the whole session — the retry after a postponed init exists only on Linux.
176	- **No `opengl32.dll` beside a harness:** the same GL 1.1 signature, in the harness only. The canvas never builds its volumes, so `Plater::select_object` returns false after `load_files`, and the workspace harness then crashed opening a paint gizmo with nothing selected (`0xC0000005`).
177	- **No `dxil.dll`:** GL initializes through `d3d12`, then the process exits with `0x80070057` on the first canvas draw, with no log line, no dialog, and no event-log entry. `slow_bootup` only moves this crash from startup to the first canvas paint.
178	
179	Confirm what actually loaded from the process's module list rather than from
180	file presence. The log names the live driver: `4.6 … llvmpipe` is Mesa's software
181	rasterizer, `4.2 … D3D12 (Microsoft Basic Render Driver)` is Mesa's `d3d12`
182	driver on WARP. A fully provisioned tree renders under both;
183	`GALLIUM_DRIVER=llvmpipe` selects the former.
184	
185	## Implementation evidence record
186	
187	For every production area, record:
188	
189	- product requirement and underlying technical nature;
190	- current Orca command owner, snapshot location, and event source;
191	- mature precedents and what each proves;
192	- remaining delta and risk category;
193	- production and upstream-owned files touched;
194	- verification command and exit condition;
195	- platforms exercised and gaps remaining;
196	- regression checks for the original Orca path;
197	- any behavior that could not be tested and why.
198	
199	## Rebase evidence
200	
201	The rules for changing upstream-owned files are in [fork stewardship](fork-stewardship.md). This section states only what a change must produce as evidence.
202	
203	A change that touches an upstream-owned file is not finished until the handback or pull request records three things:
204	
205	1. **The rebase simulation and its result**, run against current upstream with both endpoint commits named. For each conflicted file, state whether the natural resolution is correct or lossy. A lossy resolution is a defect to fix before the change lands, not a note for the person who rebases.
206	2. **Every upstream line deleted or rewritten**, with where the logic went and why an additive form was not possible.
207	3. **What the fork now owns** — product policy, a small neutral seam, or upstream behavior. The third needs an explicit justification.
208	
209	An automatic merge is evidence about today's text. It never proves that a future rebase will merge, and never proves that the merged result behaves correctly. After every upstream update, run the contract tests, the full native build, the multi-plate Prepare/Check workflow, native gizmo input, and the original-Orca regression checks.
210	
211	The incidents that produced these rules are in [OrcaSlicer integration guide](orca-integration-guide.md). Historical logs and spike procedures are in [POC reference](poc-reference.md).
